        "target": "Given:\n- Skye drove 6 kilometers in total.\n- For the first 3 kilometers, his speed was 150 km/h.\n- For the next 2 kilometers, his speed was 150 km/h + 50 km/h = 200 km/h.\n- For the last 1 kilometer, his speed was 2 * 150 km/h = 300 km/h.\n\nTo find the average speed, we need to calculate the total time taken and then use the formula: average speed = total distance / total time.\n\n1. Time for the first 3 kilometers:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}} = \\frac{3 \\text{ km}}{150 \\text{ km/h}} = 0.02 \\text{ hours}\n   \\]\n\n2. Time for the next 2 kilometers:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Time} = \\frac{2 \\text{ km}}{200 \\text{ km/h}} = 0.01 \\text{ hours}\n   \\]\n\n3. Time for the last 1 kilometer:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Time} = \\frac{1 \\text{ km}}{300 \\text{ km/h}} = \\frac{1}{300} \\text{ hours} \\approx 0.00333 \\text{ hours}\n   \\]\n\nTotal time:\n\\[\n0.02 \\text{ hours} + 0.01 \\text{ hours} + 0.00333 \\text{ hours} = 0.03333 \\text{ hours}\n\\]\n\nTotal distance:\n\\[\n6 \\text{ km}\n\\]\n\nAverage speed:\n\\[\n\\text{Average speed} = \\frac{6 \\text{ km}}{0.03333 \\text{ hours}} \\approx 180 \\text{ km/h}\n\\]\n\nFINAL: 180",
